  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/0c592ccfd6ea92e0f0181d5dfe4804cc0dba800f468d9fb4e9d96cef2be72be4.png)\n\nFind the $6$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above.",
  "worked_solution": "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}\u2061(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,\u27f9\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/ff5d1682ca8d142ad4cf73b9a5c996164653d940157efd695d1da13d34900404.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=4.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{4}={e}^{(4)\\pi \\text{i}/3}={e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
